<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dunbar (1650)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1650">1650</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dunbar was fought on 3 September 1650 near Dunbar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s English army defeated a larger Covenanter army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Leslie, which had supported Charles II. Thousands of Scots were killed or captured, and Cromwell took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. After a further defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> in 1651, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was occupied and incorporated into the Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dunbar-1650.jpg" alt="Battle of Dunbar (1650)" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Carrick Gow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cromwell_at_Dunbar_Andrew_Carrick_Gow_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the execution of Charles I on 30 January 1649, the Rump Parliament set up a republican Commonwealth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The Scottish Parliament, which had not been consulted, declared his son Charles king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, but required him first to sign both Covenants, recognising the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">the Kirk</a> in religion and of parliament in civil affairs. He accepted with the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> on 1 May 1650 and landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> on 23 June. The English sent the New Model Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, which crossed the border near Berwick on 22 July with over 16,000 men.</p><p>David Leslie's new army never reached its intended 36,000. A commission purged it of men suspected of supporting the Engagement or thought sinful, against the objections of much of the nobility and of Leslie himself, leaving it largely raw recruits. The Scots withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and stripped the land of provisions, and at the end of August Cromwell, his army weakened by disease and hunger, fell back to Dunbar. Leslie followed and took a strong position on Doon Hill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Doon Hill was exposed to foul weather and hard to supply, and on 2 September a Scottish council of war resolved to advance; the idea that the clerics present pressed Leslie into it is, according to the historian Royle among others, a myth. At about 4:00 am on 3 September English cavalry cleared the Scottish pickets from the crossings of the Brox Burn, and at first light Lambert's cavalry brigade crossed at the road ford and scattered the Scottish cavalry, some still in their tents. Hemmed in by the terrain, Leslie could not reinforce the fighting, while Cromwell used his last reserve to outflank the Scots, whose cavalry broke and fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Estimates of the Scottish dead range from fewer than 300 to Cromwell's claim of near 4,000; about 1,000 were wounded, and all accounts agree that about 5,000 prisoners were marched south. English losses were low: Cromwell put their dead at not twenty men, or at 30–40. Leslie rallied his army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a>, while the English took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a>, and Edinburgh Castle held out until December. Parliament struck the Dunbar Medal for the combatants, claimed to be the first campaign medal given to all ranks. Historic Environment Scotland lists the site in its Inventory of Historic Battlefields as a battlefield of national importance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dunbar (1650)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
